---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';
import { breadcrumbSchema, organizationSchema } from '../utils/schema';

const description =
  'Josh rents a flat, grows food on a small balcony, and writes down what works. The story behind Pot and Balcony and where it is heading.';

const personSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: 'About Josh and Pot and Balcony',
  url: `${SITE.url}/about/`,
  mainEntity: { '@type': 'Person', name: SITE.author.name, jobTitle: SITE.author.role, url: `${SITE.url}/about/` },
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Josh and Pot and Balcony"
  description={description}
  jsonLd={[personSchema, breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'About', path: '/about/' }]), organizationSchema()]}
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="container about-hero">
      <div>
        <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'About' }]} />
        <p class="kicker">About</p>
        <h1>Hi, I&rsquo;m Josh.</h1>
        <p class="lede">I rent a flat, I have a small balcony, and I&rsquo;m learning to grow food on it &mdash; properly, in public.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="about-photo">
        <!-- PLACEHOLDER: replace with a real photo of Josh -->
        <img src={SITE.author.avatar} alt="Placeholder portrait of Josh" width="400" height="400" fetchpriority="high" />
        <figcaption>that&rsquo;s me <em>(real photo soon)</em></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="page-body">
    <div class="container container--narrow">

<div class="prose">
  <h2>How this started</h2>
  <p>
    I'm 27, I live in Berlin, and I rent — which means no garden, 
    no raised beds, and a landlord who'd notice if I drilled into 
    the railing. What I do have is a south-facing balcony about 6m², 
    a windowsill that gets decent light in spring, and a genuine 
    interest in figuring out how much food you can actually grow 
    in a space like that.
  </p>
  <p>
    It started with a pot of basil that died, then another one that 
    didn't, then a tomato plant that took over the whole balcony and 
    actually produced fruit. At some point I started writing things 
    down, because I kept forgetting what worked.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most gardening content online is written for people with gardens. 
    The advice assumes outdoor taps, soil you can amend, space to make 
    mistakes in. I don't have any of that — and I suspect most people 
    reading this don't either.
  </p>

  <h2>Why Pot and Balcony exists</h2>
  <p>
    I wanted the resource I couldn't find: honest, specific advice for 
    people growing in tiny rented spaces, from someone actually doing it. 
    Real photos, real mistakes, real prices — and no pretending that an 
    expensive gadget is essential.
  </p>

  <h2>The long game: a farm</h2>
  <p>
    The balcony is my training ground. My longer-term plan is to 
    transition into small-scale farming in Germany — proper mixed 
    production, not just pots and planters. I'm doing my Ausbildung 
    to make that happen properly, not just jumping in blind.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every guide here is a small experiment in growing more with less: 
    less space, less time, less money wasted on the wrong kit. The 
    balcony teaches you discipline. A farm will need a lot of it.
  </p>

  <h2>How this site is made</h2>
  <p>
    I'll be upfront: I use AI tools to help with research, drafting 
    and publishing. I'm a marketing professional by day, so I know 
    how content sites work — and I want this one to be different. 
    What AI can't do is stand on my balcony. The photos, the field 
    notes and the verdicts on gear come from me. If I haven't tested 
    something myself, I'll say so clearly.
  </p>

  <h2>Ground rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>I only recommend gear I've actually used — or I tell you plainly that I haven't.</li>
    <li>Affiliate links are always disclosed, and they never decide what I recommend. <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Details here</a>.</li>
    <li>If I get something wrong, I'll fix it and say so.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Say hello</h2>
  <p>
    Got a question, a correction, or a balcony horror story? 
    <a href="/contact/">Get in touch</a> — I read everything.
  </p>
</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-hero { display: grid; gap: var(--s-7); align-items: center; }
  @media (min-width: 52rem) { .about-hero { grid-template-columns: 1.4fr 0.6fr; } }
  .about-hero h1 { margin-bottom: var(--s-4); }
  .about-photo { margin: 0; justify-self: center; }
  .about-photo img { width: min(100%, 16rem); aspect-ratio: 1; border-radius: 50%; border: 2px solid var(--forest); background: var(--olive); box-shadow: 8px 8px 0 var(--forest); }
  .about-photo figcaption { font-family: var(--font-hand); font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.5rem; margin-top: var(--s-4); text-align: center; }
  .about-photo em { font-style: normal; opacity: 0.8; }
</style>
